description:
FILED s513g (Opus, 2026-10-09), found while writing the #2878 fixture; NOT fixed (outside the batch).

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 prints `hi`; PCL drops the second statement, then dies at run time "statement not supported"):

    BEGIN {
      for my $n (qw(ONE)) {
        *{"main::$n"} = sub () { 1 };
      }
      *{"main::greet"} = sub { "hi" };
    }
    print greet(), "\n";

PCL: `PCL: statement dropped at f7.pl line 5: *{"main::greet"} = sub { "hi" }; -- Handle single node of unknown type.`

What does NOT reproduce (each probed): the same glob-assign at file top level after an empty `for` (f4); inside BEGIN after an empty `for` (f6); inside BEGIN alone (f5).  So the trigger is the PREVIOUS loop body's `sub () { 1 }` (an anonymous sub with an empty prototype) followed, after the loop's `}`, by `*{...} = sub {...}` in the same block.  PPI's own tree for the statement is correct (Cast `*`, Block, `=`, `sub`, Block -- dumped), so the suspect is a PCL token repair or the anon-sub `:prototype`/`()` pre-pass that re-classes tokens across the loop's closing brace.  DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (not taken): dump the token stream PCL lowers for line 5 after `_ppi_parse`'s repairs and compare with PPI's raw dump.

Probe files: s513g worktree scratch/s513g/pr/f2.pl, f4.pl..f7.pl.  The #2878 fixture `Pl/t/lib/FactsFixture.pm` orders its BEGIN block around it (comment there).
